Q: Check if a `std::string` uses small string optimization (SSO) A: <p>Tony is right that you cannot get the answer by converting the pointers to integers, even should the implementation allow that, as the the ordering of the pointees can be completely unrelated to the ordering of the converted pointers.</p>
<p>But you also cannot use the comparison operators on pointers, as comparing pointers which don't point into (or past) the same object is not sufficiently defined.</p>
<p>Luckily, using the ordering objects <code>std::less&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>std::greater&lt;T&gt;</code> and so on gives you a guaranteed full order, so let's go with that:</p>
<pre><code>constexpr bool is_sbo_mode_active(const auto&amp; x) noexcept {
    constexpr auto less = std::less&lt;const void*&gt;{};
    auto starts_before = less(std::begin(x), std::addressof(x));
    auto ends_after = less(std::addressof(x) + 1, std::end(x));
    return !starts_before &amp;&amp; !ends_after;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The above is only fooled by node-based types which keep the first and last node inside while allocating some others outside.</p>
